WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:37.917
Define a partition rule. History rows are grouped by date. This organization can exist inside one database and should not be confused with distributing ownership across independent shards.

2
00:00:37.917 --> 00:01:14.917
Create ranges. Each month has a defined range with explicit boundaries. Future data needs a matching partition or a deliberately configured default behavior.

3
00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:56.167
Ask for November. A date-bounded query reaches the planner. The engine can eliminate irrelevant ranges if its rules and the query expression permit partition pruning.

4
00:01:56.167 --> 00:02:30.000
Read the useful part. The November partition supplies the requested history. An appropriate index and query plan can still be necessary inside that partition.

5
00:02:30.000 --> 00:03:03.917
Maintain the lifecycle. Retention can operate on old partitions, but removal is a data-policy decision. Verify dependencies, recovery requirements and correct boundaries before destructive maintenance.

6
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:46.750
Know the boundary. A query without the partition key may still inspect many parts. More partitions do not automatically add machines, guarantee speed or fix one hot-row lock.
